### How do you use getPermittedSubclasses?

Call `Class.isSealed()` to check if a type is sealed, then call `getPermittedSubclasses()` to get the array of direct permitted subtypes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class PermittedDiscovery {

    sealed interface Account
            permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
        BigDecimal balance();
    }

    final record SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}
    final record CurrentAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}
    final record LoanAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("isSealed: " + Account.class.isSealed());

        Class<?>[] permitted = Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        System.out.println("Permitted subtypes:");
        Arrays.stream(permitted)
                .forEach(c -> System.out.println("  " + c.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

```text
isSealed: true
Permitted subtypes:
  SavingsAccount
  CurrentAccount
  LoanAccount
```

### Why does it return null for non-sealed types?

If the class is not sealed, `getPermittedSubclasses()` returns `null`. Always guard with `isSealed()` first.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NullCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // String is not sealed
        System.out.println("String isSealed: " + String.class.isSealed());
        System.out.println("Permitted: " + String.class.getPermittedSubclasses());
    }
}
```

```text
String isSealed: false
Permitted: null
```

### How do you discover the full leaf set recursively?

The method returns only direct permitted subtypes. To get the complete leaf set, recurse: for each subtype, if it is itself sealed, call `getPermittedSubclasses()` again.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class RecursiveDiscovery {

    sealed interface Account
            permits SavingsAccount, LoanAccount {
        BigDecimal balance();
    }

    final record SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}

    sealed class LoanAccount implements Account
            permits HomeLoan, CarLoan {
        @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    }
    final class HomeLoan extends LoanAccount {}
    final class CarLoan extends LoanAccount {}

    public static List<Class<?>> findAllLeaves(Class<?> sealedType) {
        List<Class<?>> leaves = new ArrayList<>();
        if (!sealedType.isSealed()) {
            leaves.add(sealedType);
            return leaves;
        }
        for (Class<?> sub : sealedType.getPermittedSubclasses()) {
            if (sub.isSealed()) {
                leaves.addAll(findAllLeaves(sub));
            } else {
                leaves.add(sub);
            }
        }
        return leaves;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Class<?>> leaves = findAllLeaves(Account.class);
        System.out.println("Leaf types:");
        leaves.forEach(c -> System.out.println("  " + c.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Leaf types:
  SavingsAccount
  HomeLoan
  CarLoan
```

### What happens with non-sealed branches?

For a `non-sealed` branch, the open subclasses are not recorded anywhere and can be loaded at any time. The recursion must stop at `non-sealed` types and report them as open branches.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.lang.reflect.Modifier;

public class NonSealedBranch {

    sealed interface Account
            permits SavingsAccount, PartnerProduct {
        BigDecimal balance();
    }

    final record SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}

    non-sealed class PartnerProduct implements Account {
        @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Class<?> sub : Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses()) {
            if (sub.isSealed()) {
                System.out.println(sub.getSimpleName() + ": sealed (recurse)");
            } else if (Modifier.isFinal(sub.getModifiers())) {
                System.out.println(sub.getSimpleName() + ": final (leaf)");
            } else {
                System.out.println(sub.getSimpleName()
                        + ": non-sealed (open, cannot enumerate)");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
SavingsAccount: final (leaf)
PartnerProduct: non-sealed (open, cannot enumerate)
```

### How do frameworks use this for auto-registration?

Frameworks can derive serializer mappings, validation rules, or test cases directly from `getPermittedSubclasses()`, so the configuration never drifts out of sync with the sealed `permits` list.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class AutoRegistration {

    sealed interface Account
            permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {
        BigDecimal balance();
    }
    final record SavingsAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}
    final record CurrentAccount(BigDecimal balance) implements Account {}

    // Auto-derive subtype mapping from sealed permits
    public static Map<String, Class<?>> buildSubtypeMap(Class<?> sealedType) {
        Map<String, Class<?>> map = new HashMap<>();
        if (sealedType.isSealed()) {
            for (Class<?> sub : sealedType.getPermittedSubclasses()) {
                map.put(sub.getSimpleName().toUpperCase(), sub);
            }
        }
        return map;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Class<?>> map = buildSubtypeMap(Account.class);
        System.out.println("Auto-registered subtypes:");
        map.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println("  " + k + " -> " + v.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Auto-registered subtypes:
  SAVINGSACCOUNT -> SavingsAccount
  CURRENTACCOUNT -> CurrentAccount
```

### Is the reflective data trustworthy?

Yes. The permitted set is recorded in the `PermittedSubclasses` class-file attribute and validated by the JVM at load time. It cannot be fooled by a class that was not actually permitted. The reflective view is authoritative.

```java
// The JVM validates at load time:
// 1. Each permitted subclass actually extends/implements the sealed type
// 2. Both are in the same module
// 3. The subclass declares final, sealed, or non-sealed
// So getPermittedSubclasses() is trustworthy
```

### What are common mistakes with runtime sealed class discovery?

The most common mistake is calling `getPermittedSubclasses()` on a non-sealed class and not checking for `null`, which causes a `NullPointerException`. Another error is assuming the returned array contains loaded classes; it returns `Class` descriptors that may not yet be loaded. A third mistake is relying on the reflective data for security decisions without verifying that the classes are actually sealed at the bytecode level.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class SealedDiscoveryMistakes {
    // Non-sealed class
    static class RegularClass { }

    sealed static class Account permits Account.Savings {
        static final class Savings extends Account { }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: no null check on non-sealed class
        // Class<?>[] subs = RegularClass.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        // subs.length -> NullPointerException!

        // CORRECT: null check first
        Class<?>[] subs = RegularClass.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        if (subs == null) {
            System.out.println("RegularClass is not sealed: null returned");
        }

        // CORRECT: sealed class returns non-null array
        Class<?>[] accountSubs = Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        if (accountSubs != null) {
            System.out.println("Account permitted subclasses: " + accountSubs.length);
            for (Class<?> sub : accountSubs) {
                System.out.println("  " + sub.getSimpleName());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
RegularClass is not sealed: null returned
Account permitted subclasses: 1
  Savings
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Combine reflective discovery (`getPermittedSubclasses()`) with compile-time exhaustiveness (`switch`). The reflective approach is for framework code that auto-wires based on the sealed set at startup. The switch approach is for application code that handles each variant. Together they give both automatic framework wiring and compiler-checked application logic over the same sealed hierarchy.
